  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "Traffic noise and speech significantly interfere with learning efficiency.",
    "Long-term exposure to aircraft noise can impair students' attention and behavioral skills, making recovery difficult.",
    "Intelligible background speech is more detrimental to memory.",
    "The impact of natural environment sounds and music on learning efficiency is mixed and not clearly understood.",
    "Natural environment sounds have both advantages and disadvantages when used as masking or background sounds.",
    "Music can enhance learning but may also create noise-like interference; it can be used appropriately to mask other noises.",
    "Students' learning efficiency significantly decreases as sound pressure level (SPL) increases, with the trend becoming more pronounced above 50 dBA.",
    "A longer reverberation time has a positive impact on learning efficiency in self-study rooms by blurring surrounding sounds.",
    "In daily classes, a shorter reverberation time is necessary to improve classroom speech intelligibility and learning efficiency."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Further study on long-term exposure to traffic noise, natural environment sound, and music.",
    "Lack of clarity on the relationship between long-term traffic noise exposure and learning efficiency.",
    "Mixed merit in the relationship between natural environment sound/music and learning efficiency, especially across music styles.",
    "Need to more clearly separate acoustic conditions from other disturbing factors in future research.",
    "Need to enhance studies related to speech intelligibility in rooms, including background noise levels and Speech Transmission Index (STI).",
    "Need to establish a unified and reliable sound evaluation standard, including consistent rubrics for assessing learning efficiency across different tasks.",
    "Future research should incorporate physiological indicators (blood pressure, heart rate variability, skin conductance, MRI, EEG) to provide stronger evidence."
  ],
  "risk_of_bias_assessment": null,
  "heterogeneity_sources": [
    "inconsistencies in the difficulty of selected task tests",
    "significant variations in subjects' age, gender, and personality",
    "different experimental procedures and analysis methods"
  ],
  "publication_bias": null,
  "moderators": [
    {
      "variable": "age",
      "effect": "Older students were more susceptible to noise, while younger students showed inconsistent patterns, sometimes performing better at 64 dBA, sometimes more affected by noise.",
